`let` allows reassignment; `const` doesn't. Both are block-scoped. `const` must be initialized when declared, and prevents reassignment—but if it holds an object or array, you can still mutate its contents.

```javascript
let x = 1;
x = 2; // OK

const y = 1;
y = 2; // TypeError

const obj = { a: 1 };
obj.a = 2; // OK—mutation allowed, reassignment isn't
```

Use `const` by default (prevents accidental reassignment), `let` when you know the variable will change.
